speechtext:
That this House deplores (a) that the government has shown a lack of foresight and leadership in respect of the flooding of the Canadian market with foreign goods, particularly in the areas of footwear, textiles, clothing and furniture, which has contributed a great deal in weakening Canadian industries and resulted in a number of layoffs;
(b)	that the votes allocated by the government to the Canada Works programs are not sufficient to create enough jobs to overcome the massive and persistent unemployment which is especially severe among the 18 to 30 age group seeking permanent employment, regardless of the fact that a relatively large number of projects have been approved;
(c)	that no serious action has been taken to reduce interest rates which are the major cause of unjustified price increases and to fight effectively against inflation; and
(d)	that in spite of the fact that the present administration has very candidly acknowledged that the economic methods now in use are no longer suitable, the government, because of its weakness in the face of economic and financial dictatorship, persists in applying the same obsolete economic methods which leave the Canadian population to contend with the same economic problems.
He said; Mr. Speaker, I thank you for having read this very elaborate motion. I believe this automatically saves me the trouble of reading it myself.
Mr. Speaker, for the last five years I have had the great honour of representing one of the most beautiful constituencies of the country, that of Rimouski-

The House resumed consideration of the motion of Mr. Allard:
That this House deplores (a) that the government has shown a lack of foresight and leadership in respect of the flooding of the Canadian market with foreign goods, particularly in the areas of footwear, textiles, clothing and furniture, which has contributed a great deal in weakening Canadian industries and resulted in a number of layoffs;
(b)	that the votes allocated by the government to the Canada Works programs are not sufficient to create enough jobs to overcome the massive and persistant unemployment which is especially severe among the 18 to 30 age group seeking permanent employment, regardless of the fact that a relatively large number of projects have been approved;
(c)	that no serious action has been taken to reduce interest rates which are the major cause of unjustified price increases and to fight effectively against inflation; and
(d)	that in spite of the fact that the present administration has very candidly acknowledged that the economic methods now in use are no longer suitable, the government, because of its weakness in the face of economic and financial dictatorship, persists in applying the same obsolete economic methods which leave the Canadian population to contend with the same economic problems.

They are all facts. They are one-liners but they are devastating. The Minister of State for Fitness and Amateur Sport (Mrs. Campagnolo) is talking about one-liners. That is all that is needed to destroy you people-one-liners. Let me give you the last one-liner. The Prime Minister said on October 8, 1977;
Unemployment is not as bad as some people out there want to pretend it is.
Thank you for you patience, Mr. Speaker.
